      "marks": 5,
      "answer": "$w=-\\dfrac35+\\dfrac45i$ or $w=-1$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for $w$. M1; $w=\\frac{-4\\pm\\sqrt{16-4(2-i)(2+i)}}{4+2i}$. Use $i^2=-1$. M1. Obtain one of $w=\\frac{-4-2i}{4+2i}$ or $w=\\frac{-4+2i}{4+2i}$. A1 OE. Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of their denominator. M1; need some evidence of multiplying. Obtain final answers $-\\frac35+\\frac45i$ and $-1$ (or $-1+0i$). A1. Alternative method 1: multiply the equation by $2-i$. M1. Use $i^2=-1$. M1. Obtain $5w^2+4(2-i)w+(2-i)^2$. A1 OE. Use the quadratic formula or factorise to solve for $w$. M1. Obtain the same final answers. A1. Alternative method 2: substitute $w=x+iy$ and form separate equations for real and imaginary parts. M1. Use $i^2=-1$. M1. Obtain $2(x^2-y^2)-2xy+4x+2=0$ and $x^2-y^2+4xy+4y-1=0$. A1 OE. Form an equation in $x$ only or $y$ only and solve. M1. Obtain the same final answers. A1. Total: [5]",
